"TransactionGPT (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.08939v1)\nciteturn5965view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn5962view0\",\"lineno\":108}); Total lines: 679\nL100: Multi-modal structure. The metadata fields $\\mathcal{M}\\oplus\\mathcal{E}$ (typically totaling 10 to 20 fields, mostly categorical) within the same $\\mathbf{tr}$ collectively describe the basic property of $\\mathbf{tr}$ and are highly correlated with each other. For example, the merchant category, merchant name, location, and price are all interdependent when purchasing a pair of shoes.\nL101: Moreover, some metadata fields exhibit a extremely high cardinality, e.g., merchant ID may have millions of unique values, necessitating large embedding sizes during data encoding. In contrast, the feature vector $\\mathcal{F}$ does not contain explicit transaction metadata but includes features specifically crafted for a downstream task. $\\mathcal{F}$ often consists of hundreds or thousands of scalar values (mostly numerical), each with low cardinality and thus small embedding sizes.\nL102: Accordingly, metadata and feature vectors effectively constitute two distinct data modalities, which are embedded and modeled using different strategies within TGPT. Consequently, transaction data exhibits a multi-modal-temporal-tabular (MMTT) structure in general, as shown in Fig. cite52†1 .\nL103: ### 2.2 Problem Definition\nL104: \nL105: Analogous to how LLMs are pretrained to generate future text tokens (cite43†Radford et al., 2019 ; cite60†Touvron et al., 2023 ), TGPT is trained on MMTT transaction data to generate future transaction trajectories and predict transaction properties. We formally define the problem as follows:\nL106: ###### Definition 1.\nL107: Given a set of MMTT transaction sequences $\\{\\mathcal{S}\\},\\mathcal{S}\\in\\mathcal{S}_{train}$, our goal is to train a Transformer-based model $f$ (i.e., TGPT) to encode the transaction information and learn the temporal dynamics at scale. Given the historical transaction sequences $\\mathcal{S}_{test}=[\\mathbf{tr}_{1},\\mathbf{tr}_{2},\\cdots,\\mathbf{tr}_{n}]$, an optimal TransactionGPT should be able to generate the next $t$ transactions $[\\mathbf{tr}_{n+1},\\mathbf{tr}_{n+2},\\ldots,\\mathbf{tr}_{n+t}]$.\nL108: For each generated transaction $\\mathbf{tr}_{n+i}(1\\leq i\\leq t)$, the model can also produce a predicted label $\\mathbf{L}_{n+i}$ if required by a downstream classification task.\nL109: With the data and problem formally defined, we now turn to the design of a modeling framework capable of effectively capturing the complex characteristics of MMTT transaction data. The following section first summarizes the architectural evolution of TGPT and presents the technical detail of each model component.\nL110: ## 3 Methodology\nL111: \nL112: cite61†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: TransactionGPT (TGPT) model architecture evolution. (a) Diagram of the metadata vector, feature vector, intermediate transaction embedding, and final transaction embedding. (b) TGPT-1D. (c) TGPT-2D. (d) TGPT-3D-MTF (M etadata-T emporal-F eature). (e) TGPT-3D-FMT (F eature-M etadata-T emporal).\nL113: In this section, we first summarize the model evolution path of TGPT from 1- to 3-Dimensional and innovations in modality fusion between Transformers in different dimensions (Sec. cite11†3.1 ). Then we introduce the technical details for Transformer in each dimension (Sec. cite12†3.2 ) and our novel modality fusion method (Sec. cite18†3.3 ), followed by model time complexity analysis (Sec. cite21†3.4 ) and optimization objectives (Sec. cite22†3.5 ).\nL114: ### 3.1 Model Architecture Evolution\nL115: During the process of acquiring deeper insights into MMTT data and designing more effective model components to capture the intrinsic patterns of consumer payment trajectories, the architecture of TransactionGPT has undergone a gradual evolution.\nL116: Initially, we employ a straightforward single temporal Transformer (1D), which is subsequently advanced to a structure comprising two inter-coupled Transformers for metadata and temporal modality encoding (2D), and ultimately to three inter-coupled transformers (3D) with an additional Transformer to encode features. Each stage introduces necessary sophistication to better model the underlying data characteristics, as shown in Fig. cite62†2 .\nL117: Each additional Transformer—corresponding to an added dimension (data modality) in the architecture—is introduced to more effectively encode information from its respective data modality.\nL118: TGPT-1D. Only a single Transformer is used to model the temporal dependencies between transactions along the payment trajectory $\\mathcal{S}$, as shown in Fig. cite62†2 (b). Meanwhile, the interaction among the various fields within the same transaction is captured by a tabular MLP block. Sec. cite13†3.2.1 has more details about the temporal Transformer $\\mathsf{TF}(\\mathcal{S})$.\nL119: TGPT-2D. To more effectively encode the interactions between different data fields within one transaction $\\mathbf{tr}$ (Eq. cite63†1 ), an additional Transformer is incorporated replacing the tabular MLP, resulting in a 2D transformer architecture, as shown in Fig. cite62†2 (c). In Sec. cite15†3.2.3 , we present how we process and encode different transaction metadata $\\mathcal{M}$ to facilitate their interaction through the metadata Transformer $\\mathsf{TF}(\\mathbf{tr})$.\nL120: TGPT-3D. As we adopt TGPT towards downstream tasks with domain specific features, we think a separate feature transformer $\\mathsf{TF}(\\mathcal{F})$ is necessary. The reason has two folds: First, the feature set $\\mathcal{F}$ is way larger than the metadata and entity set ($|\\mathcal{F}|\\gg|\\mathcal{M}|+|\\mathcal{E}|$); features within $\\mathcal{F}$ are highly correlated but their semantics are only related to the corresponding downstream task  (cite53†Bahnsen et al., 2016 ).\nL121: Second, metadata and downstream features have conflicting embedding requirements: high-cardinality fields like merchant ID with millions of unique values need large embedding sizes to capture their rich information, while downstream features can be effectively represented with much smaller embeddings.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPretraining Finnish ModernBERTs (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.09213v1)\nciteturn5965view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn5962view1\",\"lineno\":85}); Total lines: 701\nL66: We utilized the special token set from the original BERT cite28†Devlin et al. (2019) and adapted the tokens for repeated spaces from GPT-NeoX-20B cite48†Black et al. (2022) , which are also employed in Olmo cite49†Groeneveld et al. (2024) and ModernBERT cite41†Warner et al. (2025) . We evaluated the tokenizers’ fertilities on the validation set of the pretraining corpus. Figure cite50†1 shows the differences in the fertilities of the tokenizers among the selected reference tokenizers.\nL67: The newly trained tokenizers yielded fertilities comparable to those of other multilingual tokenizers.\nL68: Tokenizer  | Predicted optimal  | True size\nL69: --- | --- | ---\nL70: tiny  | 27,224  | 27,264\nL71: base  | 42,200  | 42,240\nL72: large  | 55,571  | 55,616\nL73: baseline  | -  | 128,000\nL74: Table 1: Tokenizer vocabulary sizes Figure 1: Larger vocabularies predictably have better fertilities compared to smaller ones.\nL75: ### 2.4 Architecture\nL76: All models are encoder models based on the ModernBERT architecture cite41†Warner et al. (2025) , and we follow most of their design choices. Deviating from the ModernBERT, we use Rotary Position Embedding (RoPE) cite51†Su et al. (2024) , $\\theta_{i}=base^{-2i/d}$, with rotary base of $base=1000000$ instead of the original 160K used in global attention layers. We chose this rotary base because it can, in theory, extrapolate to a longer context than the 160K rotary base cite52†Liu et al. (2024) .\nL77: The number of parameters is dependent on the number of encoder layers and the vocabulary size. We refer to models as \"tiny\", \"base\", and \"large\". Model names without a suffix use an optimal vocabulary size and were trained with a context length of 16K, and models suffixed by \"short\" used a vocabulary size of 128K and were trained with a context length of 8192. Details of each model are presented in Table cite53†2 .\nL78: Model  | P  | L  | H  | I  | AH\nL79: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL80: tiny  | 51M  | 6  | 768  | 1152  | 12\nL81: tiny-short  | 129M  | 6  | 768  | 1152  | 12\nL82: base  | 143M  | 22  | 768  | 1152  | 12\nL83: base-short  | 209M  | 22  | 768  | 1152  | 12\nL84: large  | 401M  | 28  | 1024  | 2624  | 16\nL85: large-short  | 475M  | 28  | 1024  | 2624  | 16\nL86: Table 2: Model design. The column \"P\" refers to the number of parameters in millions, \"L\" refers to the number of encoder layers, \"H\" refers to the encoder layer dimensionality, \"I\" refers to the intermediate fully connected feed-forward network dimensionality, and \"AH\" refers to the number of attention heads.\nL87: ### 2.5 Training\nL88: Following previous work cite41†Warner et al. (2025) ; cite54†Portes et al. (2023) , we use the masked language modeling procedure with a 30% masking rate without the next-sentence prediction cite28†Devlin et al. (2019) . We use AdamW optimizer cite55†Loshchilov and Hutter (2019) and a trapezoidal learning rate (LR) scheduler cite56†Hu et al. (2024) ; cite57†Xing et al. (2018) . This scheduler is also known as the Warmup-Stable-Decay (WSD) schedule. We follow cite41†Warner et al.\nL89: (2025) and use $1-\\sqrt{LR}$ in the LR decay phase, and we use batch size warm-up. All models were trained with the same number of optimization steps (138000), but the number of tokens varied due to different global batch sizes, device batch size variation caused by unpadding, and the number of gradient accumulation steps. The difference in input tokens was maximally 19% (tiny and tiny-short-edu), and 50% of the models were trained on an equal number of tokens.\nL90: We describe the training as a three-step process where we assume that models’ parameters are first optimized for short context representations of the tokens (stable phase), then refine the token representations for longer dependencies (context extension phase), and finally, reinforce the representations for the inputs that we believe the model will be used for in the future (annealing phase). Training hyperparameters and additional details are provided in Appendix cite21†A .\nL91: ### 2.6 Data\nL92: All models are trained with the same pretraining data mixture. The language distribution of the pretraining data used prior to the context extension and annealing is provided in Table cite58†3 . We used text datasets from diverse sources, including web crawls, news, scientific articles, classical literature, historical texts, Wikipedia, forums, and governmental sources such as policies and laws. Sources underwent various levels of pre-processing, including the removal of low-quality text or boilerplate.\nL93: We deliberately oversample the datasets considerably (median sampling factor 5, arithmetic mean 11.21, maximum 66 for the Cultura X Latin subset cite59†Nguyen et al. (2023) ) based on experience from previous studies cite60†Samuel et al. (2023) ; cite30†He et al. (2023) . Pretraining crawl data, excluding high-quality educational data used for annealing, was globally exactly deduplicated per language cite61†Mou et al. (2023) , and personally identifiable information (PII) was removed cite62†BigScience Workshop et al.\nL94: (2023) ; cite63†Myntti (2024) . Additionally, some individual datasets were PII-removed because we considered them to be high-risk data sources. A complete listing of used datasets and processing details is provided in Appendix cite22†B .\nL95: Language  | Tokens (B)  | %\nL96: Finnish  | 209.09  | 53.6\nL97: English  | 80.77  | 20.7\nL98: Swedish  | 80.09  | 20.5\nL99: Code  | 14.12  | 3.6\nL100: Cross-lingual  | 3.98  | 1.0\nL101: Northern Sámi  | 1.07  | 0.3\nL102: Latin  | 0.94  | 0.3\nL103: Total  | 390  | 100\nL104: Table 3: Pretraining data language distribution. \"B\" refers to billions.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPretraining Finnish ModernBERTs (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.09213v1)\nciteturn5965view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn5962view1\",\"lineno\":280}); Total lines: 701\nL232: Model  | MLDR\nL233: --- | ---\nL234: Monolingual Models\nL235: ---\nL236: GTE-en-MLM-large*  | 0.364\nL237: ModernBERT-large*  | 0.343\nL238: RoBERTa-base*  | 0.226\nL239: DeBERTaV3-large*  | 0.071\nL240: Multilingual Models\nL241: ---\nL242: Finnish-ModernBERT-base  | 0.218\nL243: Finnish-ModernBERT-base-edu  | 0.255\nL244: Finnish-ModernBERT-base-short  | 0.259\nL245: Finnish-ModernBERT-base-short-cpt  | 0.259\nL246: Finnish-ModernBERT-base-short-edu  | 0.208\nL247: Finnish-ModernBERT-large  | 0.302\nL248: Finnish-ModernBERT-large-edu  | 0.306\nL249: Finnish-ModernBERT-large-short  | 0.311\nL250: Finnish-ModernBERT-large-short-cpt  | 0.287\nL251: Finnish-ModernBERT-large-short-edu  | 0.302\nL252: Finnish-ModernBERT-tiny  | 0.151\nL253: Finnish-ModernBERT-tiny-edu  | 0.135\nL254: Finnish-ModernBERT-tiny-short  | 0.196\nL255: Finnish-ModernBERT-tiny-short-cpt  | 0.153\nL256: Finnish-ModernBERT-tiny-short-edu  | 0.197\nL257: mmBERT-base  | 0.072\nL258: XLM-RoBERTa-large  | 0.166\nL259: Table 8: Model performance in out-of-domain retrieval MLDR using the nDCG@10 metric. Results with \"*\" are reported by cite41†Warner et al. (2025) . The best results for each task and the best average are highlighted in bold for both monolingual and multilingual models.\nL260: Model  | CSN  | SQA  | Average\nL261: Monolingual Models In-Domain performance\nL262: ---\nL263: GTE-en-MLM-base  | 0.449  | 0.714  | 0.582\nL264: ModernBERT-large  | 0.595  | 0.839  | 0.717\nL265: Multilingual Models Out-of-Domain performance\nL266: ---\nL267: Finnish-ModernBERT-base  | 0.361  | 0.536  | 0.448\nL268: Finnish-ModernBERT-base-edu  | 0.375  | 0.515  | 0.445\nL269: Finnish-ModernBERT-base-short  | 0.377  | 0.547  | 0.462\nL270: Finnish-ModernBERT-base-short-cpt  | 0.366  | 0.550  | 0.458\nL271: Finnish-ModernBERT-base-short-edu  | 0.349  | 0.538  | 0.443\nL272: Finnish-ModernBERT-large  | 0.461  | 0.608  | 0.535\nL273: Finnish-ModernBERT-large-edu  | 0.447  | 0.612  | 0.529\nL274: Finnish-ModernBERT-large-short  | 0.467  | 0.635  | 0.551\nL275: Finnish-ModernBERT-large-short-cpt  | 0.455  | 0.619  | 0.537\nL276: Finnish-ModernBERT-large-short-edu  | 0.452  | 0.621  | 0.537\nL277: Finnish-ModernBERT-tiny  | 0.219  | 0.491  | 0.355\nL278: Finnish-ModernBERT-tiny-edu  | 0.217  | 0.488  | 0.353\nL279: Finnish-ModernBERT-tiny-short  | 0.333  | 0.549  | 0.441\nL280: Finnish-ModernBERT-tiny-short-cpt  | 0.316  | 0.534  | 0.425\nL281: Finnish-ModernBERT-tiny-short-edu  | 0.323  | 0.548  | 0.436\nL282: mmBERT-base  | 0.454  | 0.593  | 0.523\nL283: XLM-RoBERTa-large  | 0.395  | 0.562  | 0.478\nL284: Table 9: Model performance across different code tasks using the nDCG@10 metric. The best results for each task and the best averages are highlighted in bold for both monolingual and multilingual models. In-domain results, highlighted in italics, are reported by cite41†Warner et al. (2025) .\nL285: The evaluation trend was that models trained on the edu data during the annealing phase performed a bit worse on both out-of-domain retrieval tasks. This may simply reflect the fact that the baseline annealing data contained more English overall.\nL286: ## 4 Discussion and conclusions\nL287: \nL288: We reported on pretraining the Finnish ModernBERTs for languages relevant in Finland. We explored the use of edu data in the annealing phase and evaluated our models extensively. The evaluation results show that the Finnish ModernBERTs are competitive or superior to existing models.\nL289: Finnish ModernBERTs were the strongest multilingual encoder models in out-of-domain retrieval tasks. The out-of-domain retrieval performance of the other multilingual models in MLDR was more than 14 points lower than that of the leading Finnish ModernBERT model.\nL290: The 7th percentile of the MLDR documents’ lengths was 1125 tokens, the 50th percentile was 2269 tokens, and the 93rd percentile was 7720 when using a tokenizer with a fertility close to 1 in English, indicating that sequence length extrapolation and performance near the maximum sequence length remain unknown.\nL291: However, we conclude that the context extension was successful, as the models can retrieve beyond the stable phase sequence length of 1024.^{14}^{14} 14 As the 7th percentile contains 14 documents, retrieving only them in perfect rank would yield $nDCG@10=0.07$. If a system were to retrieve the 14 documents perfectly, to obtain a score of $0.307$ system would additionally need to retrieve 125 documents with rank 5.\nL292: The usefulness of the non-English edu annealing data for enhancing the model performance in Finnish and Swedish remains unconfirmed, as performance fluctuates across evaluation datasets in short context evaluations.\nL293: ## Limitations\nL294: \nL295: We employed a pretraining method that compiles findings from various preceding research. We did not have the resources to run distinct ablation trials to assess the impact of these choices (e.g., language distributions, data oversampling, rotary base). While our results demonstrate that this pretraining method can be used to train capable multilingual models, the research is limited and leaves open questions.\nL296: The models were not evaluated on the Latin or Northern Sámi benchmarks. The inclusion of Latin was not intended to develop better models for Latin itself, but rather to support the models’ representation capability in domains that utilize Latin terms. To the best of our knowledge, the only evaluation resource for Northern Sámi is Tatoeba cite103†Tiedemann (2020) , which contains 68 English sentences and Northern Sámi translations.\nL297: Evaluating an encoder model in translation is not appropriate, as the model’s generation capabilities are limited.\nL298: We evaluated the models extensively across multilingual short context NLU tasks. The models’ performance in multilingual long context NLU tasks is unknown, and we encourage practitioners to test these models on long context tasks where optimized representations of Finnish, Swedish, English, and Northern Sámi are needed.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBeyond Task-Oriented and Chitchat Dialogues: Proactive and Transition-Aware Conversational Agents - ACL Anthology (https://aclanthology.org/2025.emnlp-main.672/)\nciteturn5965view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn5963view0\",\"lineno\":60}); Total lines: 302\nL47: Abstract Correct abstract if needed. Retain XML formatting tags such as <tex-math>. You may use <b>...</b> for bold, <i>...</i> for italic, <u>...</u> for underline, <sc>...</sc> for small-caps, <tt>...<tt> for `typewriter text`, <url>...</url> for URLs, <a href=...> for hyperlinks, and <par/> for paragraph breaks.\nL48: \nL49: Verification against PDF Ensure that the new title/authors match the snapshot below. (If there is no snapshot or it is too small, consult cite0†the PDF .)\nL50: Authors concatenated from the text boxes above:\nL51: \nL52: [Input] ALL author names match the snapshot above—including middle initials, hyphens, and accents.\nL53: \nL54: [Button: Create GitHub issue for staff review]\nL55: \nL56: * * *\nL57: ##### Abstract\nL58: Conversational agents have traditionally been developed for either task-oriented dialogue (TOD) or open-ended chitchat, with limited progress in unifying the two. Yet, real-world conversations naturally involve fluid transitions between these modes. To address this gap, we introduce TACT (TOD-And-Chitchat Transition), a dataset designed for transition-aware dialogue modeling that incorporates structurally diverse and integrated mode flows.\nL59: TACT supports both user- and agent-driven mode switches, enabling robust modeling of complex conversational dynamics.To evaluate an agent’s ability to initiate and recover from mode transitions, we propose two new metrics—Switch and Recovery.Models trained on TACT outperform baselines in both intent detection and mode transition handling.\nL60: Moreover, applying Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) to TACT-trained models yields additionalgains, achieving 75.74% joint mode-intent accuracy and a 70.1% win rate against GPT-4o in human evaluation.These results demonstrate that pairing structurally diverse data with DPO enhances response quality and transition control, paving the way for more proactive and transition-aware conversational agents.\nL61: Anthology ID:\nL62:     2025.emnlp-main.672\nL63: Volume:\nL64:     cite32†Proceedings of the 2025 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing L65: Month:\nL66:     November\nL67: Year:\nL68:     2025\nL69: Address:\nL70:     Suzhou, China\nL71: Editors:\nL72:     cite33†Christos Christodoulopoulos , cite34†Tanmoy Chakraborty , cite35†Carolyn Rose , cite36†Violet Peng L73: Venue:\nL74:     cite37†EMNLP L75: SIG:\nL76:     cite38†SIGDAT L77: Publisher:\nL78:     Association for Computational Linguistics\nL79: Note:\nL80: \nL81: Pages:\nL82:     13280–13306\nL83: Language:\nL84: \nL85: URL:\nL86:     cite39†https://aclanthology.org/2025.emnlp-main.672/ L87: DOI:\nL88:     cite40†10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-main.672†doi.org L89: Bibkey:\nL90: \nL91: Cite (ACL):\nL92:     Yejin Yoon, Yuri Son, Namyoung So, Minseo Kim, Minsoo Cho, Chanhee Park, Seungshin Lee, and Taeuk Kim. 2025. cite39†Beyond Task-Oriented and Chitchat Dialogues: Proactive and Transition-Aware Conversational Agents . In Proceedings of the 2025 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing, pages 13280–13306, Suzhou, China. Association for Computational Linguistics.\nL93: Cite (Informal):\nL94:     cite39†Beyond Task-Oriented and Chitchat Dialogues: Proactive and Transition-Aware Conversational Agents (Yoon et al., EMNLP 2025)\nL95: Copy Citation:\nL96:     [Button: More options…]\nL97: PDF:\nL98:     cite23†https://aclanthology.org/2025.emnlp-main.672.pdf L99: Checklist:\nL100:     cite41† 2025.emnlp-main.672.checklist.pdf L101: cite23†PDF cite0†Cite cite41†Checklist cite0†Fix data L102: \nL103: * * *\nL104: ##### Export citation\nL105: \nL106:     @inproceedings{yoon-etal-2025-beyond,\nL107:         title = \"Beyond Task-Oriented and Chitchat Dialogues: Proactive and Transition-Aware Conversational Agents\",\nL108:         author = \"Yoon, Yejin  and\nL109:           Son, Yuri  and\nL110:           So, Namyoung  and\nL111:           Kim, Minseo  and\nL112:           Cho, Minsoo  and\nL113:           Park, Chanhee  and\nL114:           Lee, Seungshin  and\nL115:           Kim, Taeuk\",\nL116:         editor = \"Christodoulopoulos, Christos  and\nL117:           Chakraborty, Tanmoy  and\nL118:           Rose, Carolyn  and\nL119:           Peng, Violet\",\nL120:         booktitle = \"Proceedings of the 2025 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing\",\nL121:         month = nov,\nL122:         year = \"2025\",\nL123:         address = \"Suzhou, China\",\nL124:         publisher = \"Association for Computational Linguistics\",\nL125:         url = \"https://aclanthology.org/2025.emnlp-main.672/\",\nL126:         doi = \"10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-main.672\",\nL127:         pages = \"13280--13306\",\nL128:         ISBN = \"979-8-89176-332-6\",\nL129:         abstract = \"Conversational agents have traditionally been developed for either task-oriented dialogue (TOD) or open-ended chitchat, with limited progress in unifying the two. Yet, real-world conversations naturally involve fluid transitions between these modes. To address this gap, we introduce TACT (TOD-And-Chitchat Transition), a dataset designed for transition-aware dialogue modeling that incorporates structurally diverse and integrated mode flows.\nL130: TACT supports both user- and agent-driven mode switches, enabling robust modeling of complex conversational dynamics.To evaluate an agent{'}s ability to initiate and recover from mode transitions, we propose two new metrics{---}Switch and Recovery.Models trained on TACT outperform baselines in both intent detection and mode transition handling.\nL131: Moreover, applying Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) to TACT-trained models yields additionalgains, achieving 75.74{\\%} joint mode-intent accuracy and a 70.1{\\%} win rate against GPT-4o in human evaluation.These results demonstrate that pairing structurally diverse data with DPO enhances response quality and transition control, paving the way for more proactive and transition-aware conversational agents.\"\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNeural Architecture Search (NAS): Automating Model Design | Roboflow (https://blog.roboflow.com/neural-architecture-search/)\nciteturn5965view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn5963view1\",\"lineno\":45}); Redirected to URL: https://roboflow.com/blog/neural-architecture-search; Total lines: 310\nL28: Designing a cite8†neural network†blog.roboflow.com is a challenging task, requiring mixing and matching layers, activation functions, and connection patterns until a winning combination is achieved. And today’s deep learning models are often massive image models with many layers, or language models with billions of parameters, making manually designing the perfect neural network architecture much more difficult.\nL29: cite9†Neural Architecture Search (NAS)†docs.roboflow.com has emerged as an answer to this challenge. NAS techniques treat neural‑network design as a machine‑learning problem. Rather than manually building a model architecture, an algorithm searches a space of candidate architecture, and automatically discovers a configuration that meets user‑defined objectives.\nL30: An example of NAS in practice is cite10†RF-DETR†rfdetr.roboflow.com , the SOTA object detection and segmentation model. RF-DETR was created using NAS, explained in the paper cite11†RF-DETR: Neural Architecture Search for Real-Time Detection Transformers†arxiv.org . The techniques of NAS explained in the paper are also available for you to try with cite12†Roboflow NAS†blog.roboflow.com .\nL31: \nL32: cite13†Image†storage.ghost.io cite14†An overview of Neural Architecture Search†arxiv.org L33: In this blog post, you’ll learn the following:\nL34: \nL35:   * What is NAS?\nL36:   * The main components of NAS systems.\nL37:   * How NAS works.\nL38:   * The types of search spaces and strategies in NAS.\nL39:   * Why NAS is useful.\nL40: \nL41: So, let’s get started.\nL42: ## What Is Neural Architecture Search?\nL43: \nL44: Neural architecture search is a technique for automating the design of artificial neural networks. It answers the question: What is the best model for my problem and my hardware?\nL45: Given a specific task (e.g., cite15†image classification†blog.roboflow.com , cite16†object detection†blog.roboflow.com , language modeling or image cite17†segmentation†blog.roboflow.com ), a NAS algorithm searches over a pre‑defined space of possible neural‑network topologies to find an architecture that optimizes a performance metric such as accuracy, latency, or model size. There are 3 key components of NAS :\nL46: cite18†Image†storage.ghost.io cite19†NAS at a glance†arxiv.org . A search strategy picks candidate networks from a defined search space (using an encoding of each design), a performance estimator scores each candidate, and the score is fed back to guide the next picks.\nL47: ### Search Space\nL48: The search space specifies the kinds of architectures that the algorithm may consider - it's what you are allowed to change. This includes allowable operations (such as convolution, pooling, attention layers, fully connected layers, and activation functions, etc.), how these operations can be combined, and hyper‑parameters such as kernel size or number of channels.\nL49: How big is the input image? How many layers does the model have? How many object predictions does it make? How is attention applied? The search space may be global covering complete networks, or modular, where the algorithm learns reusable cells or blocks that can be stacked to build deeper models.\nL50: ### Search Strategy\nL51: \nL52: The search strategy (or controller) is how you explore different options. It's an algorithm that proposes candidate architectures from the search space and updates its proposals based on feedback. Common strategies include randomly trying combinations, systematically testing a grid, or using modern methods that learn what works better.\nL53: ### Performance Estimation Strategy\nL54: Performance estimation is how you measure whether a configuration is good. You can train the model fully or partially, or reuse weights to save time.\nL55: Specifically, performance estimation strategies speed up evaluation using proxy tasks (e.g., training on reduced datasets or fewer epochs), replacement or substitute models that predict a model’s performance from its structure, or weight‑sharing/one‑shot approaches that train a large supernetwork containing all possible operations and then evaluate different sub‑architectures using shared weights.\nL56: cite20†NAS benchmarks†www.automl.org have been introduced to provide datasets and precomputed results that allow quick performance estimation and reduce the carbon footprint of NAS.\nL57: \nL58: A supernetwork (or supernet) is a large, over-parameterized neural network that contains all possible architectures in the search space as subnetworks.\nL59: These three components: search space, search strategy and performance estimation, are the building blocks of any NAS framework. Next, we examine how NAS algorithms actually work by stepping through a typical NAS process, and surveying the most common search strategies.\nL60: ## How Does NAS Work?\nL61: \nL62: A general NAS workflow can be summarized in following steps:\nL63: \nL64: cite21†Image†storage.ghost.io cite22†NAS Workflow†arxiv.org L65: ### Step #1: Define the search space\nL66: \nL67: In this step a set of operations (e.g., convolution, pooling, recurrent layers) and possible connections among them are decided. These operations form the building blocks for candidate architectures. The search space can be extremely large if it considers all possible layer combinations, so many NAS methods restrict the search to specific templates (such as cell‑based or hierarchical structures).\nL68: ### Step #2: Generate candidate architectures\nL69: \nL70: A controller (the search strategy) proposes architectures from the search space. The controller might randomly sample, follow a policy learned by cite23†reinforcement learning†en.wikipedia.org , mutate existing architectures in an evolutionary loop, or adjust architecture parameters using gradients.\n"
